quick_facts: Mostly U₃O₈ — 70–90% uranium by weight
quick_facts: Color varies: yellow, khaki, brown, black
quick_facts: Weakly radioactive — alpha emitters, handled with gloves
quick_facts: Each drum holds roughly a tonne of uranium value
quick_facts: Cannot detonate or power anything without conversion + enrichment

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Mining of Uranium","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/mining-of-uranium"}

definition:
    Milled, concentrated uranium oxide — mostly U₃O₈ — named for its buttery color though modern versions run khaki to black. It's the checkpoint between mine and fuel cycle: leached from ore, precipitated, dried, packed into drums worth tens of thousands of dollars each. Chemically stable and only weakly radioactive, yellowcake still contains mostly uranium-238 — far from anything fissile until conversion and enrichment transform it.

debunking_snippet:
    “Yellowcake is a step toward building bombs.” As sold, it's overwhelmingly non-fissile U-238 — unusable in weapons without the entire industrial apparatus of conversion and enrichment on top.
